PET degradation methods typically include chemical (Babaei et al., 2024), thermal (Das and Tiwari, 2019), and mechanical treatments (Koshti et al., 2018). However, these approaches are often ineffective, costly, or contribute to pollution. As a result, enzymatic methods have gained interest as a more efficient and environmentally friendly alternative (Benavides Fernández et al., 2022; Ronkvist et al., 2009). Several enzymes, such as PET hydrolases, esterases, and lipases, have demonstrated the ability to hydrolyze PET (Maurya et al., 2020). PET hydrolases, also known as PETase, are PET-degrading cutinase first isolated from Piscinibacter sakaiensis (P. sakaiensis, known as Ideonella sakaiensis) (
                     Liu et al., 2023
                     ). Cutinases (EC 3.1.1.74), a subgroup of carboxylic ester hydrolases within the α/β hydrolase superfamily, hydrolyze cutin, plant cuticles, water-soluble esters, insoluble triglycerides, and PET (Soong et al., 2022). Enzymatic degradation presents a promising strategy to mitigate the environmental impact of PET waste.

The thermal behavior of free KaPETase was investigated at various temperatures above its optimal range for 5 min (Fig. 7
                        A). While free KaPETase showed high activity at about 80 °C, it maintained 80 % even at 100 °C. Furthermore, it demonstrated that the enzyme could maintain at least 50 % of its activity at moderate temperatures. In the conducted immobilization studies, the residual activities of ZIF-8@KaPETase, and ZIF-8@KaPETase/Glu were found to be 90 and 93 %, respectively, when incubated at 75 °C for 24 h while free KaPETase maintained only 50 % of its activity. (Fig. 7B). The kd values were calculated as 3.2x10−2, 8.5x10−3, and 6.5x10−3 h−1 for the free KaPETase, ZIF-8@KaPETase and ZIF-8@KaPETase/Glu, respectively (Table 2
                        ). Correspondingly, the t1/2 values were determined to be 21.6, 81.2, and 106.2 h, indicating that immobilization in ZIF-8 enhanced the thermal stability of free KaPETase by a factor of 3.8. Moreover, the stabilization factor increased by a factor of 4.9 upon crosslinking of ZIF-8@KaPETase with glutaraldehyde. These results indicate that encapsulation in ZIF-8 shields KaPETase from denaturation, while glutaraldehyde crosslinking increased enzyme rigidity and extended its half-life.
